=== Introduction
--- Psalms 38–40 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalms 38–40
sub: Rebuke me not in thy wrath • What wait I for? • He hath put a new song in my mouth
refs: Psalm 38:18; Psalm 39:7; Psalm 40:2-3
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 38
--- Psalm 38 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 38
sub: Godly sorrow
refs: Psalm 38:1

--- Rebuke me not in thy wrath
kicker: Psalm 38 · Thine arrows
scripture: Psalm 38:1-2 | mark: O LORD, rebuke me not in thy wrath; thine arrows stick fast in me
p: It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the Living God. The Lord's chastening can be fierce, and our wish should be to acknowledge our wrongdoing, and fear God.
p: Recognizing the predicaments we may find ourselves in are our own making, and calling upon the Lord to help is the only way out of whatever we have fallen into. Whatever snare we are caught in. Whatever arrows the Lord sends our way as a wake-up call, there is no relief except confession and a plea for mercy from the longsuffering God of our salvation.

--- Greater than I can bear
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 38
p: In **Genesis 4**, we find Cain being cursed with punishment from the Lord for the first murder, the blood of Cain's brother, righteous Abel, and he says to the Lord;
big: Genesis 4:13 | mark: My punishment is greater than I can bear
p: There was no remorse for what he had done, just sorrow at the consequence. But David paints the picture of a repentant sinner, recognizing that the punishment must be just, because it comes from God.

--- No rest in my bones
kicker: Psalm 38 · Because of my sin
big: Psalm 38:3 | mark: neither is there any rest in my bones because of my sin
p: Where Cain went about his day, and lied without a qualm when asked of God about Abel; *Am I my brother's keeper?*
p: In contrast, David, as a saved believer, has become unable to rest because of the guilt his sin carries. It is his iniquities that are the burden he cannot bear.

--- Too heavy for me
kicker: Psalm 38 · Mine iniquities
big: Psalm 38:4 | mark: as an heavy burden they are too heavy for me
p: Faced with his sin and the consequences, it is David's sin that worries him more.
p: We should keep this perspective. To not go the way of Cain. Notice too, that rather than seeking healing and relief from the punishment by confession and having a contrite heart, Cain's concern is for his own life. God leaves a mark on Cain to ensure he lives and suffers for it.

--- Abhor that which is evil
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 38:4
p: But we who are His are commanded to hate iniquity. Even our own. Especially our own.
big: Romans 12:9 | mark: Abhor that which is evil
big: Proverbs 8:13 | mark: The fear of the LORD is to hate evil

--- My wounds stink
kicker: Psalm 38 · My foolishness
scripture: Psalm 38:5-6 | mark: My wounds stink and are corrupt because of my foolishness
p: David sees the wounds caused by his sin as festering and infected, stinking. Literal or metaphorical, we all see the damage of sin in our lives. The harm it's caused.
p: We may not be spared the consequences, but we need not continue in the chastening that comes with unconfessed sin.

--- Feeble and sore broken
kicker: Psalm 38 · The disquietness of my heart
scripture: Psalm 38:7-8 | mark: I am feeble and sore broken
p: The loathsome disease that rots our flesh, that makes us feeble and sore broken. The disquietude of our hearts. This may be spiritual, it may be in our soul and how we feel, it may be the effect of sin in our bodies, literal and tangible.
p: Sin manifests in our hearts and in our bodies, and brings men to conviction when we fear God.

--- My friends stand aloof
kicker: Psalm 38 · Abandoned
scripture: Psalm 38:9-12 | mark: My lovers and my friends stand aloof from my sore
p: David, who in **Psalm 32** sang of the blessedness of the man whose sins are forgiven, covered and not imputed against them, is now singing of the wounds, failing strength, bitter loneliness as a result of his actions. Abandoned and betrayed.

--- As a deaf man
kicker: Psalm 38 · Betrayed
scripture: Psalm 38:13-14 | mark: But I, as a deaf man, heard not
p: In his sin, he has made friends with those that seek His life. He was deaf to their intentions. He did not speak up against them. Now he's between the reproof of God and the mischief of the wicked, and has no recourse but to seek the Lord.

--- In thee, O Lord, do I hope
kicker: Psalm 38 · Thou wilt hear
big: Psalm 38:15
p: No matter how desperate our situation, no matter how foolish or wicked we've been, no matter the cause for our misery; be it the chastening of the Lord or the betrayal of those we've given breadth to do evil, the mercies of our Father, seeking His face and His grace, are always there for those who have trusted in Him. He has promised. God Who cannot lie. Almighty God. So we know we can turn to Him.

--- I will declare mine iniquity
kicker: Psalm 38 · I will be sorry for my sin
scripture: Psalm 38:16-18 | mark: I will declare mine iniquity; I will be sorry for my sin
p: Because He is our hope. Because evildoers may magnify themselves in our failures. Because we are willing to admit we are helpless without Him. To come up halt, stumbling and falling, unable to go on. Sorrow continually before us.
p: Because we declare our iniquity, admitting our wrongdoing, being sorry for what we have done.
p: For these reasons, we can cry out to the Lord with confidence to deliver us.

--- Evil for good
kicker: Psalm 38 · Mine adversaries
scripture: Psalm 38:19-20 | mark: They also that render evil for good are mine adversaries
p: Finally, David reminds God that many of his adversaries are wicked themselves, they render evil for good, persecuting David for doing things righteously, and hating him for it.
p: Crying out for God to make haste and plead his cause, asking Him to not leave us or forsake us, reminding Him of His promises, and really just acknowledging to the all-knowing Lord that we remember them and trust in those promises.

--- Forsake me not
kicker: Psalm 38 · Make haste to help me
p: This model prayer begins with a plea to God to not judge us too harshly, for Him to know we understand, we got the message. To confess our iniquity. To abhor it. To cry out the pain we are in, and seek relief. To admit our role in our misery, even if it is the evil of others taking advantage of our failures. And reminding ourselves by reminding God of His promises and our trust in Him.
scripture: Psalm 38:21-22 | mark: Forsake me not, O LORD
center: Amen

--- Godly sorrow
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 38:18
scripture: 2 Corinthians 7:9-11 | mark: godly sorrow worketh repentance to salvation not to be repented of: but the sorrow of the world worketh death
p: This exchange perfectly encapsulates the fundamental difference between the sorrow of the world, held by Cain, and the godly sorrow, expressed by David.

--- Cain and David
kicker: Application • Godly sorrow
p: The burden Cain couldn't bear was the punishment, but he sorrowed not unto repentance. He never changed his mind about what he had done.
p: David's sorrow is godly, he cannot bear his own iniquity and hates his sin that brought him low, rather than resenting the punishment he'd received.
p: The difference between worldly sorrow that works death versus godly sorrow that works repentance to salvation, being delivered from the scourging, the ancillary consequences and from the scorners and those that plot mischief, taking advantage of our broken and contrite state.

--- The peaceable fruit of righteousness
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 38:1
scripture: Hebrews 12:11-13 | mark: it yieldeth the peaceable fruit of righteousness

=== Psalm 39
--- Psalm 39 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 39
sub: The measure of my days • What wait I for?
refs: Psalm 39:1

--- To die is gain
kicker: Psalm 39 · The vanity of life
p: **Psalm 39** is sorrowful. It marks the hardship of being in the flesh, in a fallen world and the vanity of life, as a spiritual child of God.
p: Man's days are like a vapor. The difficulty of being vexed by the filthy conversation of the wicked, and by the weakness of our mortal bodies can cause us to question the Lord, *what wait I for?* Paul felt the same tug towards death as David, *For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.*
scripture: Philippians 1:21-24 | mark: For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain

--- Vanity and vexation of spirit
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 39:5–6
p: For those of us whose hope is in the Lord to be delivered, these sentiments can become powerful. Especially in our repeated sin, our lack of control at times. Feeling the stripes of God's chastening. The effects of time. The vanity of it all.
p: Solomon, David's son certainly felt this.
big: Ecclesiastes 1:14 | mark: all is vanity and vexation of spirit
p: Here in **Psalm 39**, David begins with confession, and reveals the frustration he has with himself.

--- A bridle
kicker: Psalm 39 · My tongue
big: Psalm 39:1 | mark: I will keep my mouth with a bridle
p: David writes that he made it a point of emphasis, that he would not sin with his mouth, to take heed of what he said. Keeping his tongue as with a bridle on a mule or a horse.
p: James, the brother of Christ, writes of the importance of this in his epistle.

--- Able also to bridle the whole body
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 39:1
scripture: James 3:2-6 | mark: able also to bridle the whole body

--- These things ought not so to be
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 39:1
scripture: James 3:7-10 | mark: My brethren, these things ought not so to be

--- My sorrow was stirred
kicker: Psalm 39 · I held my peace
scripture: Psalm 39:2-3 | mark: I was dumb with silence; my sorrow was stirred
p: And it ought not be. But as David goes on to say, it can be difficult. He set out to do what was right, keeping silent even when it may have done good to speak, careful in his speech to not say the wrong thing, and in his silence, his sorrow was stirred.

--- More harm than good
kicker: Application • Silence
p: Mature Christians, especially parents with adult children, or pastors with a flock, should recognize this feeling. When we see others doing evil, or worse, people we love being led away towards it, and we know if we speak out, correct them, especially in anger, that we'd do more harm than good.
p: The situation where we aren't angry with the person, but we know what the Bible says. Where we gently suggest and correct, but withhold our own opinion because we know it will lead us to sin, and give the others cause to justify their own evil. Where we will do more harm than good.
p: *My heart was hot within me, while I was musing the fire burned*.

--- Make me to know mine end
kicker: Psalm 39 · How frail I am
big: Psalm 39:4 | mark: make me to know mine end
p: But David's first instinct is to open his mouth to the Lord, and in his sorrow and burning heart, he asks *make me to know mine end, and the measure of my days, what it is: that I may know how frail I am*. Asking, how much longer do I have to suffer this?

--- An handbreadth
kicker: Psalm 39 · Altogether vanity
scripture: Psalm 39:5-6 | mark: thou hast made my days as an handbreadth; every man at his best state is altogether vanity
p: Answering his own question, he notes the short lifespan of man, the handbreadth of years, especially compared to the eternal God. *Verily every man at his best state is altogether vanity.*
p: In the end, we know our life here is brief, and the pain we endure is nothing, especially compared to the glory to come and the eternity with the Lord.
big: Romans 8:18

--- Life is brutal and short
kicker: Application • The measure of my days
p: This perspective, the finite life vs everlasting, can be a comfort. But it also can be a sobering reminder. That when taken together, men's lives are vain. People experience needless worry, anxiety, and inner turmoil over fleeting things like the accumulations of wealth.
p: Life is brutal and short. Our own tongue betrays us. Those we see around us constantly striving for temporary gain, often at the expense of their spiritual good, only to die, taking nothing of it with them into eternity.

--- What wait I for?
kicker: Psalm 39 · My hope is in thee
scripture: Psalm 39:7-9 | mark: And now, Lord, what wait I for? my hope is in thee
p: For those of us with Heaven as our home, who knows that being absent from the body is to be present with the Lord, the futility of this world can be a sorrowful burden at times.
p: And the knowledge that the things that vex us, that the silence we take up is not only to keep us from sin, but recognizing that our complaints may well stem from how much we deserve what we get.

--- Remove thy stroke
kicker: Psalm 39 · Rebukes
scripture: Psalm 39:10-11 | mark: Remove thy stroke away from me
p: The strokes upon our back can be more than we can take. As children of God, being scourged, we can be consumed by His blows.
p: We grow old and weary from His correction. The wages of our sin is taxing, of the flesh and the heart.
p: Better to be silent, and contrite. But still, how long O Lord?

--- That I may recover strength
kicker: Psalm 39 · Hear my prayer
scripture: Psalm 39:12-13 | mark: O spare me, that I may recover strength
p: David's Psalms frequently call out in Godly sorrow, seeking restoration.
big: Psalm 51:12 | mark: Restore unto me the joy of thy salvation
p: We may frequently ask God to bring us nearer to Thee. To *spare me that I may recover strength,* to better use the time we have left, not in vain pursuits, but in service to our Savior.
p: These are the prayers we can relate to, in our broken hearted times. Comfort we can count on during tribulations. Given for our example, and praise God for understanding our infirmities, but giving us hope. Amen.

=== Psalm 40
--- Psalm 40 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 40
sub: Out of the horrible pit • A new song
refs: Psalm 40:1

--- Out of the miry clay
kicker: Psalm 40 · I waited patiently
scripture: Psalm 40:1-2 | mark: out of the miry clay, and set my feet upon a rock
p: After the somber and broken-hearted appeal of **Psalm 39**, **Psalm 40** gives us the rejoicing of those that trust in the Lord, and declares His power to all.
p: How wonderful is our patient waiting on the Lord, having called out His name for deliverance, to be rewarded by seeing Him turn towards us to save us in love!
p: He frees us from the miry pit of sin, He delivers us out of tribulation and temptations. He brings us from the shifting sands of doubt and fear, and sets us upon a rock. Our rock, Christ Jesus. The solid ground where He establishes our goings, and directs our paths. Praise Jesus for His great salvation!

--- A new song in my mouth
kicker: Psalm 40 · Many shall see it
scripture: Psalm 40:3-4 | mark: he hath put a new song in my mouth; Blessed is that man that maketh the LORD his trust
p: We who are saved have a new song in our heart, the gospel which we preach, the good news of eternal life and forgiveness in Christ, *even praise unto our God: many shall see it, and fear, and shall trust in the LORD*.
p: Is there any better news than this? Repenting from lies to trust in the Lord, and the blessings that we are given by His grace in exchange. Eternal life, His perfect word, His judgments. The Spirit of truth. The rejection of the proud. The deliverance from the evil of others. Made sons of God and joint-heirs with Jesus. Many are the wonderful works that our Lord God has done!

--- More than can be numbered
kicker: Psalm 40 · Thy wonderful works
big: Psalm 40:5 | mark: they are more than can be numbered
p: The tone of worship and blessings towards God in this prayer song of gratitude is as infectious as it is doctrinally rich.
p: The thoughts of the Lord towards us, that He even considered a wretch like me, the wonderful things He has for us cannot be numbered. There's not enough time or words in this life for us to declare all He has done for us.
p: All we can do is cry out, "Thank you!" And share this good news with others.

--- Mine ears hast thou opened
kicker: Psalm 40 · Sacrifice and offering
big: Psalm 40:6 | mark: mine ears hast thou opened
p: The blood of bulls and goats never took away sin. It may have cleansed the flesh to be in the presence of God in the temple made with hands, but to enter into His presence forever, in the Temple of Heaven, all He has ever required was faith in His promises.
big: Romans 10:17

--- Hearing they hear not
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 40:6
p: Have they not heard? The word of God has gone out to the world and not returned void. But those that harden their hearts, and stiffen their necks and refuse to obey the gospel of God.
p: As Jesus and the prophets taught:
scripture: Matthew 13:13-16 | mark: blessed are your eyes, for they see: and your ears, for they hear

--- Whosoever shall call
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 40:6
p: God has blessed us with the conviction of the Spirit, that even in the miry pit, we heard Him, and see with our eyes the mighty God, being converted and healed. Calling upon the name of the Lord.
big: Romans 10:13

--- I delight to do thy will
kicker: Psalm 40 · Thy law is within my heart
scripture: Psalm 40:7-10 | mark: I delight to do thy will, O my God; I have preached righteousness in the great congregation
p: Continuing in **Psalm 40**, rejoicing that God has given men ears to hear the truth, and the glory to God for His protection and comfort, we are called to spread this word, proclaiming it to all men.
p: We are delighted to do His will. His law written in our hearts.

--- After the inward man
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 40:8
big: Romans 7:22 | mark: I delight in the law of God after the inward man
p: Even as our flesh may be prone to wander, the outward man waging war against the spirit, we can know His righteous paths, and preach them among the beloved believers. Declaring His faithfulness and the salvation of the Lord to all who will hear. Singing His praises, letting His light shine through us, magnifying His glory. Proclaiming His great Gospel.

--- Innumerable evils
kicker: Psalm 40 · Mine iniquities
scripture: Psalm 40:11-12 | mark: mine iniquities have taken hold upon me
p: Having acknowledged all He has done for us, and encouraging ourselves and others to turn to Him for salvation through the blessing of the Lord, David's Psalm turns introspective. He confesses the sin that has beset him, and compasses him about with the repercussions of it.
p: We cannot acknowledge salvation without acknowledging what God is saving us from.

--- O wretched man that I am!
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 40:12
p: Just as David and Paul write of having the law written in their heart, and delighting in His word, they both lament the sinful flesh that requires a Savior.
scripture: Romans 7:24-25 | mark: O wretched man that I am!

--- Make haste to help me
kicker: Psalm 40 · Deliver me
scripture: Psalm 40:13-15 | mark: O LORD, make haste to help me
p: We ask that the Lord deliver us. That it is His will that be done. That those who are moved to scorn, mocking our trust in the Lord be made ashamed.
p: That God use us to shame those that wish to do us evil, to His glory.
p: Praise, thanksgiving, confession, deliverance, and destruction of those who hold the truth in unrighteousness. This is a model of prayer. That His will be done on earth as it is in Heaven.

--- The Lord thinketh upon me
kicker: Psalm 40 · The Lord be magnified
p: And in the end, the joy of His salvation overwhelming us with gratitude and praise, that such a holy and Almighty God takes heed of our prayers and notices us. Loving us so much, while we are yet sinners. Thinking of us. Providing for us. Delivering us.
scripture: Psalm 40:16-17 | mark: The LORD be magnified; yet the Lord thinketh upon me
center: Amen

